---
// Commercial kitchen cleaning — one of the THIRTEEN "by type of premises"
// leaf pages under the /commercial-cleaning/ hub (navigation.ts, "By type of
// premises" group). Built in one pass with its twelve siblings.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the thirteen. All copy below is DRAFTED,
// not supplied. See src/pages/commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning.astro's header for the launch
// blockers shared across the whole cluster.
//
// ⚠️ THE SCOPE OF THIS PAGE IS DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN ITS TITLE, and
// that is the whole editorial decision behind it. "Commercial kitchen
// cleaning" is searched by people wanting three different things:
//   1. Periodic deep cleaning of a kitchen a brigade already cleans daily.
//   2. Exhaust canopy and DUCTWORK cleaning, which in Australia is a
//      certified trade with a standard behind it and a certificate at the
//      end. See §3.
//   3. Grease trap pump-outs, which is a licensed liquid waste service.
// This page claims (1), explains (2) rather than offering it, and refuses
// (3). Every honest version of this page has to do that, because a cleaning
// company that quietly quotes a canopy clean without certification is
// selling a document it cannot issue.
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS PAGE DIFFERENT structurally:
//   - `Callout tone="warning"` on canopy certification sits BEFORE the scope
//     and is the first substantive block. Same pattern as the medical page's
//     clinical waste and the construction page's silica: name the boundary
//     before the offer.
//   - `ComparisonTable` is replaced by a `ContentGrid` of the three trades
//     people confuse. On this page the useful comparison is not TLB versus
//     other cleaners, it is which trade does which job.
//   - `ImageBand` carries the canopy frame, because a clean canopy is the
//     single image that makes the §3 argument visible.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, specific to this page:
//   - Does TLB do commercial kitchens at all? Nothing in the brand
//     foundation document says so, and this is physically harder work than
//     anything else in the cluster (caustic degreasers, hot surfaces, tight
//     hours). Confirm before it ships.
//   - Whether TLB does canopy and filter cleaning WITHOUT certification, and
//     if so how it is described. Bracketed in §4. Getting this wording wrong
//     is the biggest single risk on the page.
//   - Cool room and freezer work: whether TLB will defrost and clean them,
//     and who is responsible for moving stock. Bracketed in §4.
//   - Whether TLB has, or will get, food safety and chemical handling
//     training on record. A council inspector will not ask, but a chef will.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import ImageBand from '../../components/sections/ImageBand.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own frames are in src/assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/;
// the prompts are in that folder's IMAGE-PROMPTS.md. The canopy frame has no
// people in it on purpose: it is the outcome §3 argues about, and a person in
// the shot would read as us claiming to have certified it.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as the hub.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
// Brief §17. Southern Gold Coast was dropped from this page's town band and
// the subheading trimmed to match. Confirmed with the client as a BUILD
// REGRESSION rather than a deliberate commercial footprint decision, so the
// three-region band is restored here: BFD 2.1 requires towns named across the
// whole footprint, and the do-not-touch rules treat a trimmed list as a
// regression rather than a choice. This is the same band the commercial carpet
// and commercial pressure pages already render, so it restores consistency
// rather than inventing anything.
//
// ⚠️ THE OTHER COMMERCIAL PAGES HAVE THE SAME REGRESSION and are out of scope
// for this brief. Only commercial carpet and commercial pressure kept the
// three-region band; every other page importing `nswLocationGroups` needs the
// same audit. See the flags in commercial-kitchen-cleaning-copy-rewrite.md.
import { locationGroups, locationSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { commercialBusiness, relatedPremises, walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../../data/premises';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. Title and description are the SIGNED-OFF workbook text
// (Keyword Mapping & Metadata, sheet 1 row 32), reproduced verbatim. The old
// title stopped one word short of the mapped primary
// `commercial kitchen cleaning services` (200/mo); the old description ran to
// 235 characters, 85 over the limit and the longest of the eighteen pages
// reviewed in this project.
//
// ⚠️ NOTE WHAT THE APPROVED META GIVES UP: the ductwork boundary. The old
// description tried to set that expectation in the search result and could not
// fit it — it was being truncated by roughly its last third, which is the
// worst possible sentence to lose, because it is the one managing the
// expectation the hood secondary creates. The right home for it is the page,
// which handles it in a dedicated section (§3) plus FAQ 2. Neither needed
// changing, but the boundary now lives only on the page.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// Brief §7. The approved workbook CTA is `Get a quote`, so per Rule 4 it goes
// on every in-page button. At eleven characters it fits easily. One primary
// CTA, repeated, per BFD 9.5, which this page was already doing with a
// different label.
//
// ⚠️ CHANGE REQUEST FOR THE WORKBOOK OWNER, not blocking. `Get a quote` is the
// weakest CTA of the ten commercial pages and names nothing. The label it
// replaces, `Book a site walkthrough` (walkthroughCtaLabel in data/premises.ts,
// still used by the sibling premises pages), is materially better HERE: this
// page argues at length that a kitchen has to be walked when it is cold with a
// floor grate lifted, and that label is the argument on a button. Suggested
// replacement: `Book a kitchen walkthrough`, 26 characters, which keeps the
// stronger ask while being specific to the service.
//
// The cost of the approved wording is visible in the closing block below: its
// lead-in describes a walkthrough and the button now says "Get a quote". If
// the workbook adopts the replacement, that block reads correctly again with
// no further edit.
//
// No BFD 13.2 conflict either way: there is no `Get an instant quote` on this
// page, the pricing block promises a walkthrough and a written scope, and
// `Get a quote` asks for a quote rather than promising an instant price.

// §1 Hero. `split-single-image`, image left — mirroring the aged care page,
// and for the same reason: the reader's real question is whether this looks
// like a real commercial kitchen or a photograph of a domestic one, and the
// stainless bench answers it before the copy can.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. Same standing note as every premises page.
//
// In `hero`, on `headingLines`:
// Brief §4. The mapped primary `commercial kitchen cleaning services`
// (200/mo) appeared nowhere in the old H1, and the H1 is scored present or
// absent. The extension is the premises list, taken straight from the
// approved meta so the search result and the page agree on who this is for.
//
// The old line — the sharpest distinction on any of the ten commercial
// pages — is not cut. It opens the lead, because it works by setting two
// clauses against each other and the mapped phrase belongs to neither.
// In `hero`, on `subheading`:
// Brief §5. Rule 5's second slot, which this page did not have. Its
// extension is the TIMING rather than the H1's premises list, and "between
// services, not during them" is lifted from the page's own opening
// paragraph, so the subheading and the body say the same thing in the same
// words. It also answers the first objection a venue has to any contractor:
// lost trading hours.
// In `hero`, on `lead`:
// Brief §6. The timing phrase comes out of the lead because the subheading
// now carries it. What replaces it is the thing that actually defines the
// service and that the nightly close-down cannot do: a cold kitchen with the
// line pulled out. "Periodic deep cleaning" is dropped from the opening
// position because `commercial kitchen deep cleaning services` (50/mo) is
// better served by the FAQ block, which covers deep cleaning intervals at
// length.

// §2 Definition. Answer-target block. Paragraph two is doing the most
// important work on the page: it separates the nightly close-down (kitchen
// staff, always) from the periodic deep clean (this page) before anyone
// reads a scope, because that confusion is what makes kitchen quotes
// incomparable.

// §3 The canopy boundary. `warning`, and the first substantive block on the
// page for the same reason the medical page leads with clinical waste: what
// a contractor refuses to do badly is what a competent operator screens for.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and this is the block to check hardest:
//   - Kitchen exhaust hood and duct cleaning in Australia is carried out to
//     AS/NZS 1668.1 requirements for the maintenance of kitchen exhaust
//     systems, and a compliant clean comes with a report and a certificate
//     for the operator's records. Correct in substance. The standard is
//     NAMED but no clause is quoted, on purpose.
//   - Insurers and landlords commonly require evidence of periodic exhaust
//     cleaning, and a grease fire claim can be affected by its absence.
//     Correct in substance and worded as "commonly" rather than "always",
//     because it depends on the policy.
//   - Grease trap servicing is licensed liquid waste work with tracking
//     obligations. Correct.
//   - Nothing here states that TLB is or is not certified. That is the
//     §4 bracket and the biggest launch blocker on the page.

// §4 Scope. `ServiceBlocks variant="icon-grid"`, grouped by WHAT MAKES EACH
// PART HARD rather than by area — the grease, the water, the cold, the
// hidden, the boundary. That grouping is unique in the cluster and it is the
// one a chef recognises.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard practice, not supplied by TLB.
// Two carry real brackets and both are the difference between this page
// being honest and not.
//
// In `scopeItems`, on "Cool rooms and freezers" (description):
// ⚠️ Real bracket. Stock movement is a food safety decision, not a
// cleaning one, and it has to be the operator's call.
// In `scopeItems`, on "Filters and the visible canopy" (description):
// ⚠️ THE bracket on this page. Wording this wrong is the biggest risk
// on it. See the file header and §3.

// §5 The three trades people confuse. `ContentGrid` with `feature` cells,
// standing in for the ComparisonTable other pages carry.
//
// On this page the reader's useful comparison is NOT TLB versus other
// cleaners — it is which of three separate trades does the job they have in
// mind. Getting that wrong costs them a compliance document, which is worse
// than paying too much. The shared comparison rows would have answered a
// question nobody on this page is asking.

// §6 "Worth knowing". The lever here is that a kitchen degrades invisibly and
// on a schedule, so the argument is about frequency rather than about price.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED:
//   - Food businesses must keep premises, fixtures, fittings and equipment
//     clean and sanitary under the Food Standards Code, and councils inspect
//     against it. Correct in substance; no clause quoted.
//   - Accumulated grease in exhaust systems is a recognised fire load and a
//     recognised cause of kitchen fire spread. Correct.
//   - NO FREQUENCY IS ASSERTED as a legal requirement, because the required
//     interval for exhaust cleaning depends on cooking type and hours, and
//     stating a single number would be wrong for most readers.

// §7 Related pages. Hospitality is the nearest neighbour and breweries the
// second — a taproom kitchen is this page's reader too.
const relatedTags = relatedPremises('/commercial-cleaning/commercial-kitchen-cleaning/');

// §9 Branded FAQs, in the CUSTOMER'S voice — a chef, owner or venue manager.
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Do you do the canopy and the ducts?" (answer):
// ⚠️ Same bracket as §4. The most important answer on the page to get
// right, and it must not be softened.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Do we have to empty the cool room first?" (answer):
// ⚠️ Same bracket as §4.

// §10 Non-branded FAQs, the long-tail block.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED. Checkable claims:
//   - Food Standards Code cleanliness obligation for premises, fixtures,
//     fittings and equipment. Correct in substance; no clause quoted.
//   - AS/NZS 1668.1 covers the use of ventilation and air conditioning in
//     buildings including the maintenance of kitchen exhaust systems, and a
//     compliant clean is documented. Correct in substance.
//   - Exhaust cleaning frequency varies with cooking volume and type. Correct,
//     and no single interval is asserted.
//   - Grease trap servicing intervals are set by the local water authority or
//     council. Correct.
//   - No dollar figure anywhere, same as every sibling page.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does commercial kitchen cleaning cost?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE, same as every sibling page.

// §11 Closing CTA.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/commercial-cleaning/commercial-kitchen-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Commercial kitchen cleaning"). This file owns the layout. The
// notes above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here
// because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'commercial-cleaning/commercial-kitchen-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The label of the page's main button. It links to the quote page in Site
    // settings. Empty falls back to the site-wide walkthrough label.
    ctaLabel: section.optional(section.text),
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    canopy: section.callout,
    canopyPhoto: section.imageBand,
    scope: section.iconGrid,
    trades: section.contentGrid,
    worthKnowing: section.textBlock,
    // The pills are the other premises pages, from Site settings.
    related: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ note: section.optional(z.array(section.text)) }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The subheading falls back to the shared service-area line, and the towns
    // come from Site settings.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ subheading: section.optional(section.text) }),
    // Heading only. The paragraphs are the shared brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    brandedFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/commercial-kitchen-cleaning/',
};

const walkthroughCta = { label: page.ctaLabel ?? walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '16/9');

const tradesBlocks = page.trades.items.map((item) =>
  item.type === 'image' ? { type: 'image' as const, ...sized(item.image, '4/3') } : item,
);

const business = commercialBusiness(pageMeta.description);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="left"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    {/* §3 — the boundary, before the offer. See the file header. */}
    <Callout tone="warning" heading={page.canopy.heading} body={page.canopy.body} cta={page.canopy.cta} />

    {/* The canopy frame, given a band of its own because it is the one image
        that makes §3's distinction visible. No people in it on purpose.

        ⚠️ Source is 4:3 in a 16/9 box, so `cover` crops top and bottom.
        Checked: the canopy, the seated filters and the cooktop all survive. */}
    <ImageBand
      ratio="16/9"
      label={page.canopyPhoto.image.label}
      caption={page.canopyPhoto.caption}
      src={page.canopyPhoto.image.src}
    />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading={page.scope.heading}
      lead={page.scope.lead}
      note={page.scope.note}
      items={page.scope.items}
      cta={walkthroughCta}
    />

    <ContentGrid heading={page.trades.heading} lead={page.trades.lead} columns={3} blocks={tradesBlocks} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.worthKnowing.heading}
      body={page.worthKnowing.body}
      cta={page.worthKnowing.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.worthKnowing.backgroundImage}
    />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.related.heading}
      subheading={page.related.subheading}
      tags={relatedTags}
      note={page.related.note ?? []}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading ?? locationSubheading}
      groups={locationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    {/* Brief §19. The six questions here are about when TLB is in the
        kitchen, the canopy boundary, frequency, cool room stock, chemicals and
        what happens on contact — all pre-booking questions. The carpet and
        pressure pages already use "Booking a commercial carpet clean" and
        "Booking a commercial pressure clean" for the same block, so this
        brings the kitchen page into line with its siblings. It carries
        `commercial kitchen deep cleaning services` (50/mo) in substance
        through "deep clean", and keeps the exact primary out of a fourth
        heading slot — the H1, subheading and scope H2 have spent the three the
        ceiling allows. The second heading stays: it carries
        `commercial kitchen cleaning` as a phrase but not the mapped primary,
        because it does not contain "services". */}
    <Faq heading={page.brandedFaq.heading} lead={page.brandedFaq.lead} items={page.brandedFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
